r:scan to build up conversation history chronologically.\n   - Need to preserve both user messages and acknowledged assistant replies\n   - Use a different name than \"history\" (which is already taken)\n\n3. **Reply function**: A reusable callable that:\n   - Delivers to user via message-to-user\n   - Adds the acknowledged reply to history\n\n4. **Model requests**: Connect user events to model:request\n   - Requests must contain actual prior messages as role-tagged records\n   - System message first with reply-function calling convention\n   - Don't send until user event arrives\n\n5. **Memory feedback**: Use cell:input on memory references for feedback-value and feedback-edge\n\nLet me think about the architecture:\n\n```\nuser-ingress (record) → event:point → user-source (behavior:source) → behavior:scan (history accumulator)\n```\n\nWait, but I need BOTH user messages AND assistant replies in the history. So I need two sources feeding into one scan, or I need to merge them.\n\nActually, let me think more carefully. The history needs to be chronological. I could:\n- Have a single source that receives both user and assistant events\n- Or have two sources and merge them\n\nSince behavior:scan takes one source, I'll use a single source for the conversation history.\n\nFor user messages: user-ingress → event:point → conv-source\nFor assistant replies: message-to-user output → event:point → conv-source\n\nBut wait - \"A bare acknowledged record must be converted to a temporal occurrence before feeding a temporal source.\" So I need event:point for the assistant reply too.\n\nThe reply function needs to:\n1. Call message-to-user with the assistant record\n2. The acknowledged reply gets converted to an event:point and fed to the history source\n\nBut \"model:request and message-to-user must run in the live network, never inside a temporal transformation callback.\" So the reply function can't be inside a scan/map callback.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The reply function is a callable (network). When invoked, it:\n1. Calls message-to-user\n2. The result (acknowledged record) needs to be fed to history\n\nBut how does the acknowledged reply get into history? If the reply function is called from the live network (not inside a scan callback), then its output can be connected to an event:point which feeds the history source.\n\nLet me design:\n\n```\n(define conv-source (behavior:source))\n(define conv-history (behavior:scan conv-source seq:append (seq:empty)))\n```\n\nFor user messages:\n```\n(-> (event:point (data:get :id user-ingress) (data:get :time user-ingress) (data:record :role \"user\" :content (data:get :content user-ingress))) conv-source)\n```\n\nWait, but user-ingress is a record. I need to capture its occurrences. The dynamic-expressions-cell shows that event:point can use cell-valued arguments reactively", :finish-reason "stop", :truncated? false}